<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodora Porphyrogenita</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 980–<a href="/years/1056">1056</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodora Porphyrogenita was Byzantine empress from 1042 until her death in 1056, and sole ruler from 1055, the last sovereign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>. The youngest daughter of Constantine VIII, she refused to marry Romanos Argyros and, accused of plotting against her sister Zoë, was confined in the monastery of Petrion in 1031. A popular uprising against Michael V in 1042 brought her out to reign jointly with Zoë, until Zoë's marriage to Constantine IX passed power to him. On his death in 1055 she took the throne again and presided in the Senate herself; she died refusing to marry or name an heir, ending the dynasty's 189-year rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Theodora Porphyrogenita</b>, Byzantine empress since 1042 and sole ruler since 1055, died childless after a sudden gastric illness.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Theodora Porphyrogenita</b>, who had reigned as empress since 1042, became sole ruler of the Byzantine Empire until her death in August 1056.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
